{
  "contract": "finding-occurrence/v1",
  "id_length": 32,
  "domain_prefix": "finding-occurrence/v1\u0000",
  "null_column": "null",
  "encoding": [
    "Each field is length-prefixed: <byte length in decimal> ':' <utf-8 bytes>.",
    "The six fields, in order: producer_run_id, producer_name, pattern_id, path,",
    "start_line (canonical decimal), start_column ('null' when absent).",
    "The concatenation is prefixed with domain_prefix and hashed with SHA-256; the",
    "first id_length hex characters are the id.",
    "NOT separator-joined. Under a raw <US> join, ('a', 'b<US>c') and ('a<US>b', 'c')",
    "produce the SAME preimage -- two different occurrences under one id. That pair",
    "is pinned below and really did collide under the recipe this replaces.",
    "The digit-boundary pair is a guard rather than a past defect: (12, 3) and",
    "(1, 23) were distinct while the numerics were separator-joined too, and would",
    "collide the moment someone emitted them unframed. Framing removes the question",
    "for every field at once, instead of forbidding a byte in a path."
  ],
  "notes": [
    "The gate matters more than the hash. An occurrence id is computed only when the",
    "run, the producer, the pattern and an UNAMBIGUOUS physical anchor are all known.",
    "producer_version, config_digest and source_commit may be null without blocking.",
    "A missing start_column is a degradation (physical-anchor-missing:start-column),",
    "not automatically a blocker -- a line-only anchor still identifies an occurrence",
    "while it stays unique within the producer run. It becomes a blocker exactly when",
    "two records share it. There is no ordinal tiebreaker: an ordinal would make",
    "identity depend on emission order, the same defect that disqualifies the SARIF",
    "ownAudit/v1 fingerprint as an identity.",
    "A digest of the SARIF bytes is NOT a run id -- that value is input_digest, a",
    "provenance field. Two runs can serialize identically; two serializers of one run",
    "can differ."
  ],
